    "answer": {
      "short": "The series diverges. However, extending its partial-sum formula to negative integer values of $n$ gives $S_n\\to-2$ as $n\\to-\\infty$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=\\frac{4(1-3^n)}{1-3}\\\\S_n&=2(3^n-1).\\end{aligned}$$\nFor the original series, $n$ is a positive integer and $S_n\\to+\\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty$, so there is no limiting sum.\n\nIf the partial-sum formula is extended to negative integer values of $n$, then $3^n\\to0$ as $n\\to-\\infty$, giving\n$$\\lim_{n\\to-\\infty}2(3^n-1)=-2.$$\nThus $-2$ describes the extended formula in the negative direction; it is not the sum of $4+12+36+\\cdots$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
